# `math::Function::pow`

[Back to `math::Function`](../Function.md)

Raises a function to a power, where the power is a number or another function.

## Signature

```cpp
math::Function math::Function::pow(const math::Function &rhs) const;
math::Function math::Function::pow(double rhs) const;
```

## What It Does

`f.pow(p)` builds `x ↦ f(x)^p`. With a number it is the ordinary power: `pow(2.0)` squares, `pow(0.5)` takes the square root, `pow(-1.0)` is the reciprocal. With a function exponent it is the general power `f(x)^g(x)`, which is how `x^x` and `(1 + x)^(1/x)` are written. Both forms differentiate exactly.

The power is real-valued, so it has a domain. A negative base with a non-integer exponent has no real value and is refused at evaluation, naming the operation; integer exponents work for any base. For complex-valued functions fractional powers use the principal branch.

## Parameters

`const math::Function &rhs`
: The exponent as a function. Both operands must come from the same variable trace (built inside one `math::function(...)` callable, or derived from one another), or one of them must be a constant. Functions from unrelated `function(...)` calls cannot be combined directly; use [`compose`](compose.md) to substitute one into the other.

`double rhs`
: The exponent as a number.

## Returns

A new `Function` whose value is the power.

## Errors

- A negative base with a non-integer exponent raises a domain error at evaluation.
- Functions from different traces are refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Integer and fractional numeric exponents, with the derivative rule `(xᵖ)′ = p xᵖ⁻¹`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function cube = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(3.0); });
  const math::Function root = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(0.5); });
  const math::Function inverse = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(-1.0); });
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({1.0, 4.0, 9.0});
  out.writeln("x^3:   ", cube({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("x^0.5: ", root({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("x^-1:  ", inverse({x}).items<double>());
  out.writeln("(x^3)' at 2 =", operators::differential("x")(cube)(2.0).item<double>(), " (3 x^2 = 12)");
}
```

### Example 2

A function exponent. `x^x` has the derivative `x^x (ln x + 1)`, which the operator produces exactly.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function xx = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(x); });
  const math::Function d = operators::differential("x")(xx);
  const double x = 2.0;
  out.writeln("x^x at 2     =", xx(x).item<double>());
  out.writeln("(x^x)' at 2  =", d(x).item<double>());
  out.writeln("x^x (ln x+1) =", std::pow(x, x) * (std::log(x) + 1.0));
}
```

### Example 3

Domain: a negative base with a fractional exponent has no real value and is refused at evaluation, while integer exponents of a negative base are fine.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function half = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(0.5); });
  const math::Function square = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.pow(2.0); });
  out.writeln("(-3)^2:", square(-3.0).item<double>());
  try {
    (void)half(-3.0);
  } catch (const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln("(-3)^0.5:", text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

